[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-64218"] :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $2\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $2\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=2.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2R\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n2=2R\\,⟹\\,R=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $1\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-79494/selection/fcd32f74ec33961a2e758326a3db6dad3c2fac3b4b27772a4354d832285fd55f" :answer/id #uuid "da8d6bb4-728f-5cd9-8269-66e598d37822" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79494/selection/a457cbc8e8f6bc1a9285b5150b046f82a66b52ae9e5552acd246736432c70d1f" :answer/id #uuid "1374676a-8d6e-5aa5-8305-21473638d1c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79494/selection/4fec99fc4a9141173b3054423d799014cbb82993957d0c6202f115942207fce7" :answer/id #uuid "9a6bb57e-e80d-579f-a04f-713b9a99a3a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79494/selection/17a585f7e449097654e6203a1cecfc345eb0add5b0dd21896ece23319aa2c53b" :answer/id #uuid "98062e33-0dea-51f2-96db-92c1aa49b6a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79494/selection/604e0a67320a59d8fbb8672d382c92fe490b634a2193108ccfdbeeecad061fd3" :answer/id #uuid "da9558f3-4792-56ac-9f97-33600fcb73ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79494/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "155056e4-6b01-569c-9f98-173a1e551d36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-79494/selection/604e0a67320a59d8fbb8672d382c92fe490b634a2193108ccfdbeeecad061fd3" :answer-field/choices ["q-79494/selection/fcd32f74ec33961a2e758326a3db6dad3c2fac3b4b27772a4354d832285fd55f" "q-79494/selection/a457cbc8e8f6bc1a9285b5150b046f82a66b52ae9e5552acd246736432c70d1f" "q-79494/selection/4fec99fc4a9141173b3054423d799014cbb82993957d0c6202f115942207fce7" "q-79494/selection/17a585f7e449097654e6203a1cecfc345eb0add5b0dd21896ece23319aa2c53b" "q-79494/selection/604e0a67320a59d8fbb8672d382c92fe490b634a2193108ccfdbeeecad061fd3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79494" :question/id #uuid "dcd4b923-bea2-5168-a3b2-3077cdbbb018" :question/math-academy-id "q-79494" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with simple harmonic motion, is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+81y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with a velocity of $3\\,\\text{m/s},$ find the amplitude of the oscillation." :question/worked-solution "The simple harmonic motion (SHM) equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the amplitude, and $ϕ$ is the phase. In our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=81\\,⟹\\,\\omega =9.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nWe're given the initial condition $y(0)=0.$ Substituing this into the equation gives\n\n$$\nR\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=0\\,⟹\\,ϕ=0.\n$$\n\nSo our general solution reduces to\n\n$$\ny=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the velocity is $3\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0,$ which means that ${y}^{′}(0)=3.$ Differentiating the general solution with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=9R\\operatorname{cos}⁡9t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n3=9R\\,⟹\\,R=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amplitude is $\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{m}.$ The solution for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡9t.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-79494/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dedcee-452d-50ff-844e-5648be3066b4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79494"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94275/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "17be7e3a-a5d6-525e-b2df-f105798b2da8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94275/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "3b9435ae-b2f1-5774-a3a2-0d1e42ec7c4e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94275/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "4f96029f-2cb7-5400-bf61-9b113149ed9c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94275/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "2df1b856-55d8-52ce-a1a1-1f87a657c8ab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94275/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "75466738-745c-5eb1-a2ef-f5ca9e1c3915" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94275/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "aa1cf199-0396-5cda-aee3-5066f446bb2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94275/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-94275/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-94275/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-94275/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-94275/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-94275/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94275" :question/id #uuid "ff5f471e-8a86-5bf8-982b-2ec38a9cfd12" :question/math-academy-id "q-94275" :question/problem "A particle is moving with simple harmonic motion. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nNote that $R,{ϕ}_{1},{ϕ}_{2},A$ and $B$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The SHM equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to the SHM equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$\n\nTherefore, only function III could be used to represent $y(t).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-94275/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e17fe7fc-3b85-5f2c-aa69-2336b05b16b2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94275"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94286/selection/a5b1b6149e4280494257bf7203e3885812e833e63f04ce1f9221093da67be0c9" :answer/id #uuid "37ae2556-09e2-51b4-95c8-1d13ff0c7c4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{2}y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94286/selection/33a1fb1a4d9d21a184e0f53a290c4fb493d791e959a9d854128a5c50cbcf1d33" :answer/id #uuid "bb4712b3-9847-53df-9bbc-5b7d47195bb1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{3}y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94286/selection/8c3aaeed78265acd047f0fbb637a57ec18ee40b2a91a6db8e599a5920d305f3a" :answer/id #uuid "c1f8097a-239f-5bdc-a054-5e5a695a7505" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94286/selection/07abd435dcc6af0e7634dc70737c56db0e85bfedd852c229abc7990ee1e977c6" :answer/id #uuid "e2838c98-1d1f-51a7-9301-0b95c70e13d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{6}y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94286/selection/ddbe92f9ac476919a2785f09409f89281624860d9b7e8be2630e25829db8cca1" :answer/id #uuid "2ca27d28-8e16-5677-a575-fddb6d45e7d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{y}^{″}+9y=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94286/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fa783c74-d3a9-5a57-a529-880c8bfab050" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94286/selection/8c3aaeed78265acd047f0fbb637a57ec18ee40b2a91a6db8e599a5920d305f3a" :answer-field/choices ["q-94286/selection/a5b1b6149e4280494257bf7203e3885812e833e63f04ce1f9221093da67be0c9" "q-94286/selection/33a1fb1a4d9d21a184e0f53a290c4fb493d791e959a9d854128a5c50cbcf1d33" "q-94286/selection/8c3aaeed78265acd047f0fbb637a57ec18ee40b2a91a6db8e599a5920d305f3a" "q-94286/selection/07abd435dcc6af0e7634dc70737c56db0e85bfedd852c229abc7990ee1e977c6" "q-94286/selection/ddbe92f9ac476919a2785f09409f89281624860d9b7e8be2630e25829db8cca1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94286" :question/id #uuid "ccb94392-acad-518a-b317-b59ffe1a3a81" :question/math-academy-id "q-94286" :question/problem "A particle of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=18\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{18}{3}=6.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6y=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94286/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94286"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94287/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "9adf627e-e147-58a0-b5eb-ab1dbe8670cb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94287/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "115cd997-b71b-55b2-87b1-321d1fc8e334" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94287/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "7bbadcd2-446b-58bb-a5dc-944482bffeb4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94287/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "338b903c-62dd-5ae2-86ca-830414dfe7cd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94287/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "b5a7c864-ec43-5073-8381-13050f5d4620" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94287/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e6d0598a-f5c3-5cf8-9157-36af83280b75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94287/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-94287/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-94287/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-94287/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-94287/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-94287/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94287" :question/id #uuid "934892af-2aea-587f-95fb-a008f0e0a602" :question/math-academy-id "q-94287" :question/problem "A particle of mass $6\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=24\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0$\n- The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n- The general solution can be expressed as $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{24}{6}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{2}{2\\pi }=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n- $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =2$ in form 1 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is true.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-94287/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94287"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
